export const SAY: Record<string, string> = {"c01":"More sold. A thinner margin. How? From July to September, Asian Paints' Decorative Business in India grew its volume by six point four percent. The company's own slide calls the growth resilient.","c02":"Yet for Asian Paints on its own, without its subsidiaries, the gross margin fell from forty three point seven percent to forty point three. Down three hundred and thirty nine basis points. A basis point is a hundredth of a percentage point.","c03":"So which part of a moat showed up here? The power to raise prices and keep the margin? Or the reach that keeps buyers buying, even after prices rise?","c04":"Asian Paints' managing director and chief executive, Amit Syngle, describes the trade himself. Quote: we calibrated price increases and absorbed part of the cost inflation to protect consumer demand in this challenging environment.","v01":"First, the buyers. Did they keep buying after the price rises? The company's chart says yes. Decorative volume grew six point four percent this quarter, after nine percent from April to June. The slide's title: resilient volume growth despite steep price hikes and festive season timing.","v02":"In money, the business grew faster: fourteen point five percent. In the same quarter last year it was the other way round. Volume grew ten point nine percent, and value only six. This year, dividing value growth by volume growth leaves about seven point six percent for price and product mix together. Our arithmetic. The filings do not split the two.","v03":"And the reach? The deck says the distribution footprint widened further, strengthening reach and depth across markets. It gives no count of dealers, depots or tinting machines. So the reach itself cannot be measured from these filings.","m01":"So what is gross margin, in money? Picture a hundred rupees a customer paid. A year ago, after paying for materials, about forty three rupees seventy paise was left. This quarter, about forty rupees thirty paise. Our arithmetic, from the company's own statement.","m02":"We rebuilt it from the audited statement. Materials consumed, plus goods bought for resale, adjusted for the change in stock, come to five thousand and twenty eight crore rupees, against four thousand, one hundred and twenty nine a year ago. That is fifty nine point seven percent of sales, against fifty six point three. It matches the deck's forty point three and forty three point seven.","m03":"Total material cost rose twenty one point eight percent. Sales rose fourteen point eight. Had the margin held at last year's level, about two hundred and eighty six crore rupees more would have been left this quarter. Our arithmetic, and only an illustration of scale.","p01":"So did the company raise its prices? Yes. Slide twenty four puts small boxes over each quarter. April to June: material, up about twenty five percent. Price, up about seven. July to September: material, up about three. Price, up about five. The slide does not say what each is measured against.","p02":"Slide twenty four's note says, quote, calibrated pricing interventions, about twelve percent, partially offset the impact. Twelve is seven plus five, added, our arithmetic. So, about twelve percent across the two quarters. The slide does not state the base. And the company's own word is partially.","p03":"And the margin fell quarter by quarter: forty five point six percent in January to March, forty three point eight in April to June, forty point three now. The slide's reason is higher-cost stock flowing through, after steep inflation from April to June. Slide twenty five adds lower price pass-through. In plain terms, pass-through is how much of a cost rise reaches the price.","o01":"So how did the next profit line fall less? P B D I T, the company's profit before depreciation, interest, tax and other income, still rose five point nine percent in rupees. Its margin slipped from eighteen point five percent to seventeen point one. Other expenses grew only four point three percent, falling from about eighteen rupees twenty to sixteen rupees fifty of every hundred. Our arithmetic. The statement names nothing inside that line; the deck credits cost optimisation initiatives.","g01":"Did the rest of the group escape the margin squeeze? The industrial slide is titled: strong industrial growth continues, lower pass-through dents margins. International profit before tax fell thirty one percent, and its slide says the entire impact of steep inflation was felt during the quarter. One exception: bath fittings, which swung to a profit before tax of eight point seven crore rupees, against a loss of four crore a year earlier.","b01":"But. Here is the strongest case against reading this as weakness. Amit Syngle says the company absorbed part of the cost, quote, to protect consumer demand. If that was a choice, giving up margin to keep volume growing is the moat being used, not lost. The filings give the company's word for that, but no way to test it.","b02":"It may also be timing. Slide twenty four says higher-cost stock was still flowing through after April to June. On that slide, material was up about twenty five percent in April to June, and about three in July to September, with no base given. Stock on the balance sheet rose to six thousand and nine crore rupees, from four thousand eight hundred and twenty five in March. The filings do not say what that stock cost.","b03":"But the company itself points the other way. Amit Syngle, quote: with inflationary pressures showing little sign of easing, we are implementing a further round of staggered price increases in the coatings business. And its outlook slide says competitive intensity remains elevated.","b04":"What the filings do not say. No market share. No competitor named. Nothing that links lower pass-through to rivals. And the call transcript is not filed yet. The company says it will be uploaded.","e01":"So, back to the tin. Volume up six point four percent. Of every hundred rupees, about forty rupees thirty paise left after materials, down from forty three seventy, our arithmetic. Our reading: the buyers kept buying, so the volume test was passed. The price test, only partly. In the company's own word, partially.","e02":"The next test is the December quarter. Where does the gross margin go from forty point three? And does volume keep growing through the next round of price increases?"};
